    "lawCode": "Amd §§120, 133 & 134, Ec Dev L; amd §311, Exec L; amd §163, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Economic Development Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A4097\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the economic development law, in\nrelation to membership of the small-business advisory board and the\nminority and women-owned business advisory board; and to amend the\neconomic development law, the executive law, and the state finance law,\nin relation to the powers and duties of the division for small-business\nand the division of minority and women's business development\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: To improve the outreach efforts of the\nDepartment of Economic Development's Divisions for Small Business and\nMinority and Women's Business Development.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: Sections 1 and 2 add individuals repres-\nenting banking, community development financial, insurance or surety\nbonding institutions to both the Minority and Women-Owned Business\nEnterprise Advisory Board and the Small Business Advisory Board. Section\n3 removes an outdated reference to a repealed section of law and\nrequires the Division for Small Business' information and outreach\nprogram for potential state contractors to include information about\nsurety bonding requirements. Sections 4 and 5 expand the powers and\nduties of the Division for Small Business, and the Division of Minority\nand Women's Business Development, in providing and facilitating access\nto programs that provide business development assistance. Section 6\nrequires the Office for General Services to include information about\nsurety bonding requirements in their annual regional training programs\nfor minority and women-owned businesses.\n\nEFFECTS OF PRESENT LAW WHICH THIS BILL WOULD ALTER:  Increases member-\nship of advisory boards; specifies inclusion of information on surety\nbonding in training programs for potential state contractors; augments\npowers and duties of Division for Small Business and Division of Minori-\nty and Women's Business Development.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: In testimony at Assembly hearings and in published\nreports, small and minority and women-owned businesses have repeatedly\nexpressed the need for better access to working capital, insurance and\nsurety bonding. Adding representatives from the financial community to\nthe Small Business and Minority and Women-Owned Business Advisory Boards\nwill facilitate communication between these two sectors. This legis-\nlation will also augment the role of the DED Divisions for Small Busi-\nness and Minority and Women's Business Development as gateways for\naccess to the complex and confusing network of state, federal and local\nprograms that are potential sources of business development assistance,\nincluding financing resources such as loans, loan guarantees, and surety\nbond guarantees. To conserve scarce state resources, it asks DED to seek\nto maximize the utilization of federal resources available to small,\nminority and women-owned businesses.  Finally, to address one of the\nbarriers to increased participation by small and minority and women-\nowned businesses in state contracts, this legislation ensures that the\nstate's outreach programs for small and minority and women-owned poten-\ntial state contractors include information on access to surety bonding.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  2008: A.2329-A/S.5202-A\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately, provided that the required appointments\npursuant to sections 1 and 2 of the Act shall be made by April 1, 2010.\n\n",
